**Read:** [Calibration of aerosol instruments — Chen, Fletcher and Cheng](../sources/Aerosol-Instrument-Calibration-Chen-Fletcher-Cheng.lingua#r_03PDE8353KQ9HQKJNAHRH99NPP), sampling and instrument response; revisit [A specimen, a sampling target, and a result about a population](../../../inquiry/metrology/foundations/Sampling-and-representativeness.lingua#r_0F0VEC42GPQPRZM0S7MXF7GAEH).

Sampling changes what reaches the detector. A tube can lose particles to its wall, a container can contribute residue, a swab can recover only a fraction of the target and a sample can change during transport. Include those paths in the measurement model rather than assuming that everything observed originated in the product.

Location and timing also matter. A measurement during idle conditions may miss the exposure created by handling or an intervention. Taking many samples from one convenient place does not remove the uncertainty about unobserved places.

**Work:** Draw a complete sample path and label possible loss, introduction and change. Compare a spatially distributed design with repeated sampling at one location. Explain the populations to which each result could reasonably apply.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
